    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "The irregular layer, named last time — taught this time",
      "body": [
        "The first look at إِعْرَاب named two irregular layers without teaching them: the five nouns, and the sound plurals. Both swap a short-vowel ending for a longer one — this lesson shows exactly how.",
        "الْأَسْمَاء الْخَمْسَة (\"the five nouns\") — أَب، أَخ، حَم، فَم، ذُو — use وَ for رَفْع, ا for نَصْب, and ي for جَرّ when they're the first term of an iḍāfa: أَخُو الطَّالِبِ (رَفْع), أَبَا الطَّالِبِ (نَصْب), أَخِي الطَّالِبِ (جَرّ). With an attached pronoun instead (أَخِي on its own means \"my brother\"), the three cases collapse to one identical-looking form — the long vowel only shows up clearly with a following NOUN, not a pronoun.",
        "The sound masculine plural swaps ـُونَ (رَفْع) for ـِينَ in BOTH نَصْب and جَرّ — the two other cases share one marker. The sound feminine plural stays regular everywhere except نَصْب, where it unexpectedly borrows its own جَرّ ending (kasra) instead of the fatḥa a regular noun would take."
      ],
      "previewSymbols": ["أَخُو / أَبَا / أَخِي", "ـُونَ / ـِينَ", "ـَاتٌ / ـَاتٍ"]
    },
